			<div class="content" id="post-content-2171147">
									

<p>This is far from bad, it just needs polish. Most of the issues I mention below are grammar or vocabulary based (other users are covering the rest). The amount of Russian (and the cyrillic alphabet) being used is probably a stylistic thing, but I get the feeling that some of this has been translated (wild guess, but a handful of words/phrases mistranslating would explain most of these issues).</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Mobile Task Force Sigma-3</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think this is a typo. It's the only mention of this task force.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>nomadistic</p>
</blockquote>
<p>nomadic</p>
<p>EDIT: Googled it after the fact. Nomadistic may be correct depending on context, but every spell check I try says it's wrong. Most of what I see on the academic end is nomadic (with nomadistic being used occasionally in translations), and nomadistic seems to mostly be used by those who don't know the term nomadic and as a username by at least one person.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>capable of communication through speech in varying dialects</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This may just be an issue with my vocabulary, but I was under the impression that dialects were like regional variations of the same language. While the footnote addresses what language/dialects are typically used, this seems like it should be worded differently with the footnote handling the specifics (it kind of does already).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Progress Engineering</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Foundation shells typically end up with the acronym SCP, and those in other languages either have that acronym or take it on when translated into English. I don't know if this is enforced or just what most authors default to as an in-joke, but I figured I'd bring it up. It isn't affecting my vote.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Interview Excerpt: SCP-2595-399</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I was going to bring up some grammar issues in this, but then I realized that most of them may be intentional for stylistic reasons. If this was intentional, good job.</p>
<p>That being said, there are some from the English speaking researcher that are a little off.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You do seem to understand me. Does the usage of English make you uncomfortable?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Drop the &quot;do&quot; in the first sentence and change &quot;usage&quot; to use.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I do think that it is clear for your species</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Similar issue with the &quot;do&quot; here. I've rarely seen it used in this manner except where emphasis was desired.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I am sure i will have plenty of time to be remembered by the fellows of my kind due to a superior contribution. Unfortunately, i do not seem to be able to foresee the same future within your empty prophesying.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the doctor slipping into what I thought was a stylistic choice being used by 399. Specifically, the lack of &quot;I&quot; capitalization. While this could be a method of indicating something about the manner in which 399 speaks, a native English speaker would likely not have the same speech peculiarities.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

